== 5.8 Chapter 5 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [The unit of composition in poetry is],
  [the chapter],
  [the line],
  [the paragraph],
  [the sentence]
)

#mcq(2, [A sentence that runs past the end of a line is an example of],
  [a caesura],
  [a refrain],
  [end-stopping],
  [enjambment]
)

#mcq(3, [A line whose sense and grammar close at the line end is],
  [free verse],
  [end-stopped],
  [enjambed],
  [run-on]
)

#mcq(4, [A pause within a line, often marked by punctuation, is],
  [an anapaest],
  [a caesura],
  [a refrain],
  [a volta]
)

#mcq(5, [The first step in scanning a line is to],
  [look for rhyme],
  [count the syllables],
  [mark the stressed syllables],
  [count the words]
)

#mcq(6, [A foot of one unstressed followed by one stressed syllable is],
  [a trochee],
  [a dactyl],
  [an anapaest],
  [an iamb]
)

#mcq(7, [A foot of one stressed followed by one unstressed syllable is],
  [a trochee],
  [an anapaest],
  [a spondee],
  [an iamb]
)

#mcq(8, [“Shall I compare thee to a summer's day” is written in],
  [trochaic tetrameter],
  [iambic pentameter],
  [dactylic hexameter],
  [anapaestic trimeter]
)

#mcq(9, [A line of five metrical feet is called],
  [pentameter],
  [hexameter],
  [trimeter],
  [tetrameter]
)

#mcq(10, [A spondee is],
  [two stressed syllables],
  [three unstressed syllables],
  [a missing stress],
  [two unstressed syllables]
)

#mcq(11, [Blank verse is],
  [unrhymed but metred, usually in iambic pentameter],
  [rhymed but unmetred],
  [unrhymed and unmetred],
  [rhymed and metred]
)

#mcq(12, [Free verse is characterised by],
  [a fixed stanza length],
  [the absence of a regular metre and rhyme],
  [regular rhyme],
  [the use of prose paragraphs]
)

#mcq(13, [A fourteen-line poem in iambic pentameter with a turn is],
  [an ode],
  [a sonnet],
  [a ballad],
  [an elegy]
)

#mcq(14, [A Petrarchan sonnet divides into],
  [four tercets],
  [two sestets],
  [an octave and a sestet],
  [three quatrains and a couplet]
)

#mcq(15, [In a Shakespearean sonnet the couplet usually],
  [abandons the metre],
  [repeats the opening],
  [introduces a new rhyme],
  [turns or resolves the argument]
)

#mcq(16, [A poem of mourning for a named person is],
  [a pastoral],
  [an elegy],
  [a ballad],
  [an ode]
)

#mcq(17, [A dirge is best distinguished from an elegy by],
  [its use of rhyme],
  [its subject],
  [its length],
  [its ritual, performed and repeated quality]
)

#mcq(18, [A long narrative poem on a heroic scale, with a nation at stake, is],
  [a ballad],
  [an ode],
  [an epic],
  [a lyric]
)

#mcq(19, [A mock epic works by],
  [applying epic machinery to a trivial subject],
  [parodying a ballad],
  [imitating an epic badly],
  [shortening an epic]
)

#mcq(20, [A dramatic monologue is a poem in which],
  [the poet speaks in his own voice],
  [two characters converse],
  [a character speaks at a critical moment to a silent listener],
  [a chorus comments]
)

#mcq(21, [A pastoral poem presents],
  [a shepherd's biography],
  [a farming manual],
  [country life as simpler and better],
  [city life as corrupt]
)

#mcq(22, [A praise poem, such as the Yoruba *oriki*, works chiefly by],
  [accumulating epithets that name the subject's essence],
  [dialogue],
  [narrating a life in order],
  [rhyming couplets]
)

#mcq(23, [An image in poetry is],
  [any comparison],
  [language that appeals to the senses],
  [a rhyme],
  [a symbol]
)

#mcq(24, [A symbol differs from a metaphor in that a symbol],
  [states a comparison directly],
  [must be an animal],
  [is always explained],
  [carries meaning without asserting it]
)

#mcq(25, [An extended and elaborate comparison sustained through a poem is],
  [a conceit],
  [an apostrophe],
  [a refrain],
  [a symbol]
)

#mcq(26, [Addressing a dead person or an inanimate object directly is],
  [personification],
  [hyperbole],
  [apostrophe],
  [apostasy]
)

#mcq(27, [“The wind wept through the empty house” uses],
  [litotes],
  [simile],
  [metonymy],
  [personification]
)

#mcq(28, [Pathetic fallacy is],
  [a logical error in the argument],
  [an exaggerated claim],
  [a false metaphor],
  [nature given the feelings of the scene]
)

#mcq(29, [Rhyme scheme letters follow],
  [the length of the lines],
  [the stanza number],
  [the sound of the rhyme],
  [the spelling]
)

#mcq(30, [An eye rhyme is a rhyme in which],
  [the spelling matches but the sound does not],
  [the stress falls on the first syllable],
  [only the vowels match],
  [the sounds match but the spelling does not]
)

#mcq(31, [A rhyme in which the stress falls before the final syllable is],
  [slant],
  [masculine],
  [feminine],
  [internal]
)

#mcq(32, [Alliteration is the repetition of],
  [final consonants],
  [initial consonants],
  [whole words],
  [vowel sounds]
)

#mcq(33, [Assonance is the repetition of],
  [vowel sounds],
  [initial consonants],
  [rhythm patterns],
  [final consonants]
)

#mcq(34, [Onomatopoeia occurs when],
  [the line rhymes internally],
  [two stresses collide],
  [the word imitates the sound it names],
  [the poem addresses an object]
)

#mcq(35, [A refrain in a poem],
  [replaces the rhyme],
  [repeats a line at intervals],
  [reverses the metre],
  [introduces a new speaker]
)

#mcq(36, [The volta of a poem is],
  [its first line],
  [its rhyme scheme],
  [the point at which its argument turns],
  [its final refrain]
)

#mcq(37, [Equal stanzas of equal length most often suggest],
  [order, balance and argument],
  [a song],
  [free verse],
  [disorder]
)

#mcq(38, [A poem of four lines rhyming *abcb*, alternating four and three stresses, is a],
  [couplet],
  [tercet],
  [sonnet],
  [ballad stanza]
)

#mcq(39, [The most reliable first move on an unseen poem is to],
  [scan every line],
  [read the last two lines, then the first two],
  [count the lines],
  [look for the poet's name]
)

#mcq(40, [“The poet uses alliteration to emphasise” is a weak answer because],
  [it does not say what sound is repeated or what it does to the line],
  [it quotes the wrong line],
  [alliteration is not a device],
  [poems do not use alliteration]
)

